# 15. The big picture

You can think of backend communication as several generations of abstraction:

```text
Raw networking
     ↓
TCP sockets
     ↓
HTTP
     ↓
REST APIs
     ↓
RPC frameworks
     ↓
Microservice communication
     ↓
RPC + messaging + events
```

And at an even higher level:

```text
                Distributed System
                       │
          ┌────────────┴────────────┐
          ↓                         ↓
     Synchronous                Asynchronous
          │                         │
          ↓                         ↓
     REST / RPC              Message Queue
          │                         │
      ┌───┴───┐                ┌────┴────┐
      ↓       ↓                ↓         ↓
    REST    gRPC            Kafka     RabbitMQ
```

### The one-sentence definition

> **RPC is a mechanism that makes invoking a procedure on a remote computer feel similar to invoking a local procedure, while the framework handles networking, serialization, and request/response mechanics.**

For your Java backend studies, **gRPC is the modern RPC technology worth learning first**, while **RMI is useful mainly for understanding Java's historical approach to distributed objects**.
